        "evidence": "Definition permits an empty internal-edge set: take one vertex, its four distinct slots, and four external half-edges attached bijectively to those slots. The one-vertex underlying graph is connected. Then V=1, I=0, E=4 and the displayed definitions give L=0 and omega_D=0. The empty vertex set itself is excluded by the explicit nonempty vertex hypothesis.",
        "case_scope": {
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "source_excerpt": "Let $D\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge1}$ be the number of Euclidean momentum coordinates. Use the finite undirected multigraph convention of [[def-multigraph-and-digraph-degrees-and-connectivity]]: loops are allowed, distinct edges with the same endpoints are parallel, and connectedness here means that the underlying simple graph obtained by deleting loops and identifying each parallel class is connected.\n\nA **finite quartic graph** is data $\\Gamma=(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal E_{\\rm int},\\eta,\\mathcal H_{\\rm ext},(S_v)_{v\\in\\mathcal V},a)$ with the following properties. The vertex set $\\mathcal V$ is finite and nonempty, the internal-edge set $\\mathcal E_{\\rm int}$ and external-half-edge set $\\mathcal H_{\\rm ext}$ are finite, and $\\eta:\\mathcal E_{\\rm int}\\times\\{0,1\\}\\to\\mathcal V$ assigns the two (unoriented, but separately labelled) ends of each internal edge to vertices. Its endpoint set is $\\{\\eta(e,0),\\eta(e,1)\\}$, a singleton for a loop; distinct elements of $\\mathcal E_{\\rm int}$ with the same unordered endpoints remain distinct parallel edges. The internal multigraph $(\\mathcal V,\\mathcal E_{\\rm int},\\eta)$ is connected in the sense just specified.\n\nFor every $v\\in\\mathcal V$, the incidence-slot set $S_v$ has exactly four elements. Set $\\mathcal S:=\\bigsqcup_{v\\in\\mathcal V}(\\{v\\}\\times S_v)$. The attachment map\n\n$$a:(\\mathcal E_{\\rm int}\\times\\{0,1\\})\\sqcup\\mathcal H_{\\rm ext}\\longrightarrow\\mathcal S$$\n\nis a bijection, and for each internal end $(e,j)$ its slot has vertex coordinate $\\eta(e,j)$. Thus the two ends of a loop occupy distinct slots. Each external half-edge $h\\in\\mathcal H_{\\rm ext}$ is attached by $a(h)$ to exactly one vertex slot and has no second endpoint. In particular, each vertex has exactly four slots and every slot is used exactly once by an internal-edge end or an external half-edge. Write $V:=|\\mathcal V|$, $I:=|\\mathcal E_{\\rm int}|$, and $E:=|\\mathcal H_{