items/lem-qsmx-finite-group-irrep-dimension-square-sum.md:91:3.1 For matrices $A=\sigma(h)$ and $B=\pi(h^{-1})$ in chosen bases, the coefficient of a rectangular matrix unit $E_{ij}$ in $AE_{ij}B$ is $A_{ii}B_{jj}$. Summing these diagonal coefficients gives $\operatorname{tr}(T\mapsto ATB)=\sum_{i,j}A_{ii}B_{jj}=\operatorname{tr}A\operatorname{tr}B=\chi_\sigma(h)\overline{\chi_\pi(h)}$. Linearity of the diagonal sum yields $\operatorname{tr}P=|H|^{-1}\sum_h\chi_\sigma(h)\overline{\chi_\pi(h)}$. Since $P^2=P$, every $T$ splits as $P(T)+(T-P(T))\in\operatorname{im}P\oplus\ker P$; the intersection is zero because $P$ is the identity on its image. In a basis adapted to this direct sum, $P$ has matrix $\operatorname{diag}(I,0)$, so $\operatorname{tr}P=\dim\operatorname{im}P$. An intertwiner between irreducibles is either zero or an isomorphism because its kernel and image are invariant. By [F4]–[F5], $\mathbb C$ is algebraically closed, so [F6] gives every endomorphism $T$ of the nonzero finite-dimensional space $V_\pi$ an eigenvalue $\alpha$. For $T\in\operatorname{End}_H(V_\pi)$, the eigenspace $\ker(T-\alpha I)$ is nonzero and $H$-invariant because $T$ intertwines the $H$-action; irreducibility forces it to be all of $V_\pi$, so $T=\alpha I$. Thus $\dim\operatorname{im}P$ is $1$ if $\pi\cong\sigma$ and $0$ otherwise, proving character orthogonality by the averaging calculation. [given, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, step 2.2, algebra]
