+## Example
+
+Let $G=\\mathbb Z_2=\\{0,1\\}$ with addition modulo $2$, and define $\\omega:G^3\\to\\mathbb C^\\times$ by $\\omega(a,b,c)=(-1)^{abc}$. Let $\\operatorname{Vec}_{G}^{\\omega}$ have as objects finite-dimensional complex $G$-graded vector spaces, as morphisms grade-preserving linear maps, tensor product
+$$(W\\otimes U)_c=\\bigoplus_{a+b=c}W_a\\otimes_{\\mathbb C}U_b,$$
+and unit $\\delta_0$, the one-dimensional line in grade $0$. Write $\\delta_a$ for the one-dimensional line in grade $a$. On homogeneous lines take the associator
+$$\\alpha_{\\delta_a,\\delta_b,\\delta_c}=\\omega(a,b,c)\\operatorname{id}_{\\delta_{a+b+c}},$$
+and extend it linearly to direct sums. Since $\\omega$ is a normalized $3$-cocycle, this defines a finite semisimple fusion category with $\\delta_a\\otimes\\delta_b\\cong\\delta_{a+b}$. In particular, $\\delta_1\\otimes\\delta_1\\cong\\delta_0$, while $\\alpha_{\\delta_1,\\delta_1,\\delta_1}=-\\operatorname{id}_{\\delta_1}$. The untwisted choice $\\omega\\equiv1$ has the same fusion multiplicities but associator $+\\operatorname{id}_{\\delta_1}$ on that triple. Thus the fusion multiplicities alone do not record the specified associator component; no claim about monoidal inequivalence of these two categories is made.
+
+## Example references
+
+[Etingof, Gelaki, Nikshych, and Ostrik, *Tensor Categories*, Example 2.3.8 and §4.1](https://math.mit.edu/~etingof/egnobookfinal.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** $G=\\mathbb Z_2$ under addition modulo $2$, complex finite-dimensional graded vector spaces, and the displayed function $\\omega$.
+
+[F1] [[def-aqft-noninvertible-defect-fusion]]: finite semisimple fusion data include a finite set of simple-object labels, a simple tensor unit, fusion multiplicities, an associator satisfying the pentagon, unitors satisfying the triangle, and duality maps satisfying the snake identities.
+
+[F2] [[def-monoidal-category]]: a monoidal category has a tensor bifunctor, unit object, natural associator and unitors; the associator obeys the pentagon and the unitors obey the triangle.
+
+## Verification
+
+1.1 For $a,b,c,d\\in\\{0,1\\}$, the exponent of $-1$ on the left side of the $3$-cocycle equation is
+$$(a+b)cd+ab(c+d)\\equiv abc+abd+acd+bcd\\pmod 2,$$
+while on the right it is
+$$abc+a(b+c)d+bcd\\equiv abc+abd+acd+bcd\\pmod 2.$$
+Hence $\\omega(a+b,c,d)\\omega(a,b,c+d)=\\omega(a,b,c)\\omega(a,b+c,d)\\omega(b,c,d)$. If any argument is $0$, then $\\omega=1$, so the cocycle is normalized. [given, algebra]
+
+2.1 Every graded object is a finite direct sum of copies of $\\delta_0$ and $\\delta_1$, so the category is semisimple with exactly these two simple isomorphism classes; the unit $\\delta_0$ is simple. The tensor product preserves grading and sends $\\delta_a\\otimes\\delta_b$ to $\\delta_{a+b}$. On each homogeneous component the associator is multiplication by the scalar $\\omega(a,b,c)\\in\\{1,-1\\}$, so it is invertible and natural with respect to every grade-preserving linear map. On a tensor product of four homogeneous lines, the two pentagon composites have scalar factors given by the two sides of the cocycle equation in step 1.1, so they agree. Normalization makes the unitors identities, and the triangle then holds. The dual of $\\delta_0$ is $\\delta_0$ with identity evaluation and coevaluation. For $\\delta_1$, choose itself as left and right dual, coevaluation scalar $1$, and evaluation scalar $-1$; each snake composite has scalar $1\\cdot(-1)\\cdot(-1)=1$ because $\\omega(1,1,1)=-1$. Direct sums inherit duals, so the category has all properties required by [F1]. [F1, F2, step 1.1, given, algebra]
+
+2.2 Since $1+1=0$ in $G$, $\\delta_1\\otimes\\delta_1\\cong\\delta_0$. But $\\omega(1,1,1)=(-1)^1=-1$, so the associator on the triple $(\\delta_1,\\delta_1,\\delta_1)$ is multiplication by $-1$; for the untwisted function it is multiplication by $+1$. Both fusion tables have $N_{11}^{0}=1$ and all other $N_{11}^{a}=0$, so these integers do not by themselves specify that associator component. This comparison concerns the written structure data only and makes no equivalence claim. [F1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
