        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $S_3=\\langle r,s\\mid r^3=s^2=1,\\ srs=r^{-1}\\rangle$ be the permutation group on three letters, and let $\\zeta=e^{2\\pi i/3}$. On $V=\\mathbb C^2$ with basis $u,v$, define $r(u)=\\zeta u$, $r(v)=\\zeta^2v$, $s(u)=v$, and $s(v)=u$. Let $\\mathbf 1$ be the trivial representation and let $\\varepsilon$ be the one-dimensional sign representation, with $\\varepsilon(r)=1$ and $\\varepsilon(s)=-1$. In the finite-dimensional complex representation category with the tensor product action $g\\cdot(x\\otimes y)=(g\\cdot x)\\otimes(g\\cdot y)$, these objects satisfy\n$$V\\otimes V\\cong\\mathbf 1\\oplus\\varepsilon\\oplus V.$$\nThe category is a finite semisimple fusion category, $V$ is simple, and $V$ has no tensor inverse. This is an abstract representation-theoretic example and asserts no realization as a defect in a quantum field theory.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
