          "def-monoidal-category": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $G=\\mathbb Z_2=\\{0,1\\}$ with addition modulo $2$, and define $\\omega:G^3\\to\\mathbb C^\\times$ by $\\omega(a,b,c)=(-1)^{abc}$. Let $\\operatorname{Vec}_{G}^{\\omega}$ have as objects finite-dimensional complex $G$-graded vector spaces, as morphisms grade-preserving linear maps, tensor product\n$$(W\\otimes U)_c=\\bigoplus_{a+b=c}W_a\\otimes_{\\mathbb C}U_b,$$\nand unit $\\delta_0$, the one-dimensional line in grade $0$. Write $\\delta_a$ for the one-dimensional line in grade $a$. On homogeneous lines take the associator\n$$\\alpha_{\\delta_a,\\delta_b,\\delta_c}=\\omega(a,b,c)\\operatorname{id}_{\\delta_{a+b+c}},$$\nand extend it linearly to direct sums. Since $\\omega$ is a normalized $3$-cocycle, this defines a finite semisimple fusion category with $\\delta_a\\otimes\\delta_b\\cong\\delta_{a+b}$. In particular, $\\delta_1\\otimes\\delta_1\\cong\\delta_0$, while $\\alpha_{\\delta_1,\\delta_1,\\delta_1}=-\\operatorname{id}_{\\delta_1}$. The untwisted choice $\\omega\\equiv1$ has the same fusion multiplicities but associator $+\\operatorname{id}_{\\delta_1}$ on that triple. Thus the fusion multiplicities alone do not record the specified associator component; no claim about monoidal inequivalence of these two categories is made.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-generated"
        },
        "sources": {
